      "turn": 1,
      "doctor_question": "Hello, I'm Dr. Smith. It's nice to meet you. Could you tell me what brings you in to see me today?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S011",
      "patient_response": "Nice to meet you, Dr. Smith. Well, I suppose what brought me in is... I just keep getting up. Like, in meetings or at dinner, when everyone else is seated and settled. I'll suddenly feel like I need to go refill my coffee, or walk to the back of the room to check a handout, even though nothing is really urgent at all. \n\nWe have these incredibly long staff meetings at the school—I teach tenth-grade history, which I usually love because the kids are finally old enough to understand real historical nuance, you know? Though this year the administration changed our textbooks and it has been an absolute headache trying to adapt my lesson plans. I spent practically all weekend just redoing my unit on the Industrial Revolution. \n\nBut anyway, yes, I'll just stand up in the middle of all that. Or even at home during dinner. My husband made this wonderful pot roast on Sunday—he’s been watching that new British cooking show and trying out all the recipes, which is nice for me because I don't have to cook as much—and I just kept popping up from the table for no real reason...",
